3
00:00:10,633 --> 00:00:17,077
The absolute value of x minus four equals
six means x minus four is six away from

4
00:00:17,077 --> 00:00:19,145
zero, in either direction.

5
00:00:19,595 --> 00:00:26,765
So x minus four equals six, or x minus
four equals negative six. x equals ten, or

6
00:00:26,765 --> 00:00:27,915
negative two.

7
00:00:29,033 --> 00:00:35,349
Always isolate the absolute value first.
Three times the absolute value of two x

8
00:00:35,349 --> 00:00:38,271
plus one, minus five, equals sixteen.

9
00:00:38,721 --> 00:00:45,301
Add five and divide by three: the absolute
value of two x plus one equals seven. So

10
00:00:45,301 --> 00:00:51,563
two x plus one is seven or negative seven,
and x equals three or negative four.

11
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:58,867
When the other side has a variable, check
every answer. The absolute value of x plus

12
00:00:58,867 --> 00:01:00,849
two equals two x minus one.

13
00:01:01,299 --> 00:01:07,278
The cases give x equals three, or x equals
negative one third. But at negative one

14
00:01:07,278 --> 00:01:11,945
third, the right side is negative, so only
x equals three works.

15
00:01:13,067 --> 00:01:19,119
For less than, the solution is an and: the
absolute value of x minus one is less than

16
00:01:19,119 --> 00:01:23,819
four means negative three is less than x,
which is less than five.

17
00:01:24,269 --> 00:01:30,352
For greater than, it's an or: the absolute
value of x minus one is greater than four

18
00:01:30,352 --> 00:01:34,914
means x is less than negative three, or x
is greater than five.

19
00:01:36,033 --> 00:01:38,679
An absolute value can never be negative.

20
00:01:39,129 --> 00:01:44,931
So the absolute value of x equals negative
two has no solution, and the absolute

21
00:01:44,931 --> 00:01:49,283
value of x is greater than negative two is
true for every x.

22
00:01:50,433 --> 00:01:55,575
Your turn. Solve the absolute value of two
